# The fifth road panel (TSC_Road_Last check spot, Perception 14). The
# unfinished road. Two readings: before the courier's handover the man
# is still walking overhead; after it, the road has ended and the stone
# is owed its last chisel strokes.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_RoadLast

entry stopped if flag(TSC_MetCourier)

== stopped ==
The fifth panel is the plainest of the five, and the sharpest eye in the company is the one that sees why: it is not plain. It is unfinished. The rider is carved walking, not riding, and the road under his feet simply stops where the chisel marks stop.

Sixteen centuries this wall has waited for its last strokes.

* [Look closer at the walker.] -> walker_stopped

== walker_stopped ==
The walker's face is worn past reading, but his shoulders are not: they are carved carrying something small, held to the chest with both hands, as if it were the last thing he owned.

He stopped, on the turf overhead, and handed it to the party, and sat down facing the way he came.

The road on this stone has an end now. The stone just has not been told.

* "Somebody should finish the carving." -> finish

== finish ==
Perhaps somebody will. Four bars of a song and a foot of chiselwork, both waiting on the same event, both owed to men who carried further than anyone should be asked to.

Whatever the company does at the bottom of this barrow is the last stroke.

* [Move on.] -> end

== start ==
The fifth panel is the plainest of the five, and the sharpest eye in the company is the one that sees why: it is not plain. It is unfinished. The rider is carved walking, not riding, and the road under his feet simply stops where the chisel marks stop.

The other four roads were cut whole, eight hundred years before their riders ever left, and every one of them came true. This one the carver left open on purpose.

* [Look closer at the walker.] -> walker

== walker ==
The walker's face is worn past reading, but his shoulders are not: they are carved carrying something small, held to the chest with both hands, as if it were the last thing he owned.

He is not history. He is somewhere out there right now, still walking, and this stone is waiting to find out how he ends before it commits to an ending.

* "Then the fifth road is not finished." -> open

== open ==
Not finished, and not abandoned: the chisel marks stop mid-stroke, tools-down. Work interrupted, not ended, by an answer that had not arrived yet.

Whatever the company does with what it is carrying, this wall intends to be right about it.

* [Move on.] -> end
